# Build context for Dockerfile.tests only (BuildKit uses <Dockerfile>.dockerignore in preference to
# the shared .dockerignore, which the production images keep using unchanged).
#
# An allowlist: everything is excluded, then only what the offline suite reads is let back in.
# Local secrets and state (.env, .env.*, .aws/, data/, .venv/, .git/, logs, caches) are never listed,
# so they cannot enter the image or its layers even if new ones appear. Later rules win.
*

# Application and tests (the suite also runs scripts/ and reads the static page under src/).
!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!src
!tests
!scripts

# Repository files that tests assert on: container and deployment definitions, workflows, docs,
# the governance and contributor records, and the portraits the team page checks.
!compose.yaml
!Dockerfile.*
!.dockerignore
!.gitignore
!requirements.txt
!requirements-kepler.txt
!certs
!deploy
!images
!docs
!.github
!AGENTS.md
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!CONTRIBUTORS.md
!CLA-ACCEPTANCES.md
!CLA-EXCEPTIONS.md

# Re-exclude generated files and any secret-looking file inside the allowed directories.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/.aws

# Last, so the rules above cannot hide it: the documented placeholder template only. It holds
# names with empty values and no secrets, and the shipped-configuration tests read it. A
# developer's real .env (or .env.local and so on) is never copied.
!.env.example
